    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the common terms of the arithmetic progressions, we first note that the first term of each progression is $2$ and $3$ respectively, and that the common difference of the first progression is $1$, while the common difference of the second progression is $7$. Thus, we look for the smallest positive integer $n$ such that the terms can be expressed as:\n\n$$\n(2 + 1(n-1), 3 + 7(n-1)) < 500\n$$\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n$$\n(n-1 + 2, 7n) \\lt 501\n$$\n\nTherefore, the smallest $n$ that satisfies this inequality is $n=98$, giving us the common terms:\n$$\nn-1 + 2 = (98-1) + 2 = 98 \\quad \\text{terms}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common terms form an arithmetic progression with the $98^{\\text{th}}$ term being $97$, and the $99^{\\text{th}}$ term being $8$. Considering that the common difference is $7$, the largest common term less than 500 is $\\boxed{497}$.",
